          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nRigidification is a process for removing a flat subgroup from the inertia.\nFor example, if <MATH_0001> is a projective variety, the morphism from the Picard\nstack to the Picard scheme is a rigidification of the group of automorphism\n<MATH_0002>.\n<STRUCT_0002>\n<STRUCT_0003> Abramovich, Corti, Vistoli:\n<EMPHOPEN_0001>Twisted bundles and admissible covers<EMPHCLOSE_0001> <REF_0001>\n<STRUCT_0004>\nLet <MATH_0003> be an algebraic stack over <MATH_0004> and <MATH_0005> be a flat, finitely\npresented separated group scheme over <MATH_0006>. Assume that for every object\n<MATH_0007> there is an embedding\n<MATH_0008> which is compatible\nunder pullbacks in the sense that for every arrow <MATH_0009>\nover <MATH_0010> and <MATH_0011>, <MATH_0012>.\nThen there exists an algebraic stack <MATH_0013> and a\nmorphism <MATH_0014> which is\nan fppf gerbe such that for every <MATH_0015>, the morphism\n<MATH_0016>\nis surjective with kernel <MATH_0017>.\n<STRUCT_0005>\n<STRUCT_0006> Romagny: <EMPHOPEN_0002>Group actions on stacks and applications<EMPHCLOSE_0002>\n<REF_0002>\n<STRUCT_0007>\nDiscusses how group actions behave with respect to rigidifications.\n<STRUCT_0008>\n<STRUCT_0009> Abramovich, Graber, Vistoli:\n<EMPHOPEN_0003>Gromov-Witten theory for Deligne-Mumford stacks<EMPHCLOSE_0003> <REF_0003>\n<STRUCT_0010>\nThe appendix gives a summary of rigidification as in <REF_0004> with two\nalternative interpretations. This paper also contains constructions for\ngluing algebraic stacks along closed substacks and for taking roots of line\nbundles.\n<STRUCT_0011>\n<STRUCT_0012>\nAbramovich, Olsson, Vistoli: <EMPHOPEN_0004>Tame stacks in positive characteristic<EMPHCLOSE_0004>\n(<REF_0005>)\n<STRUCT_0013>\nThe appendix handles the more complicated situation where the flat subgroup\nstack of the inertia <MATH_0018> is normal but not\nnecessarily central.\n<STRUCT_0014>\n<STRUCT_0015>\n\n",
